Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.80,0:00:04.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}What if Earth's magnetic field… simply vanished?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.24,0:00:08.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}It is the invisible shield around the whole planet.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.75,0:00:12.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}And every day, it quietly saves your life.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.20,0:00:18.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}In Reykjavik, the northern lights are the brightest anyone has ever seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.02,0:00:23.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}In Tokyo, a fishing fleet follows one small needle home through the fog.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.57,0:00:28.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}In Alberta, migrating geese circle the same frozen field all night.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.88,0:00:33.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Every sea turtle loses the line it has followed its whole life.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.35,0:00:37.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}At the South Pole, one research station goes quiet.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.16,0:00:41.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}And nobody, anywhere, feels a thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.11,0:00:43.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Then.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.34,0:00:48.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After one hour, every compass on Earth spins in circles.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.03,0:00:50.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Sailors fall back on the stars.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.66,0:00:54.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After one day, the aurora dies over Iceland.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.41,0:00:57.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}The sky above the poles is only sky.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.54,0:01:02.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After one week, satellites begin to die in orbit.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.61,0:01:06.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}One by one, the world loses its eyes.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.28,0:01:10.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After one month, the power grids begin to fail.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.91,0:01:15.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Transformers arc in the dark, across three continents.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.27,0:01:18.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After one year, the air itself starts to leave.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.99,0:01:22.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}The sky thins to a hard, empty blue.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.29,0:01:24.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}And the sun burns straight through.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.87,0:01:28.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Cracked fields dry under that hard light.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.99,0:01:32.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After a hundred years, the seas are shallow and warm.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.81,0:01:35.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Rivers end in white salt.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:35.37,0:01:39.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}After a thousand years, the last water is gone.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.08,0:01:43.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}This is the part nobody wants to say out loud.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.17,0:01:45.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Our planet becomes Mars.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.49,0:01:46.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Cold.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.53,0:01:47.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Silent.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.68,0:01:49.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Unbreathable.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.13,0:01:53.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}A dry wind drags red dust over the empty cities.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.41,0:01:56.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Nobody is left to look up at the stars.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.31,0:01:59.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}But here's the twist.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.29,0:02:02.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}This has already happened, right next door.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.40,0:02:06.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Mars once had a magnetic field as strong as ours.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.08,0:02:09.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Four billion years ago, that field died.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.40,0:02:12.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}And the solar wind stripped its air into space.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.97,0:02:18.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Its oceans, its warmth, its sky… all of it, simply blown away.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.17,0:02:22.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}And our own shield is leaking already.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.06,0:02:26.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}In the last two hundred years, it lost about nine percent of its strength.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.87,0:02:32.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Above the South Atlantic there is a real weak spot, and satellites are hit as they cross it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.97,0:02:36.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}The Space Station carries extra shielding to fly through it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.83,0:02:41.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Hubble closes its sensitive eye whenever it passes over that gap.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.99,0:02:43.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}So remember.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.35,0:02:45.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}That shield is not a wall.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.54,0:02:50.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}It is a slow, living thing, and it is already changing.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.09,0:02:54.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}Mars lost its field first, and then it lost everything else.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.60,0:02:56.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}So here is my question.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.62,0:03:01.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(150,150)}If you could feel it fading… what would you do with the time you have?
